     After the RADIUS request has been constructed, it is sent either by means
     of rraadd__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt() or by a combination of calls to
     rraadd__iinniitt__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt() and rraadd__ccoonnttiinnuuee__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt().

     The rraadd__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt() function sends the request and waits for a valid
     reply, retrying the defined servers in round-robin fashion as necessary.
     If a valid response is received, rraadd__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt() returns the RADIUS
     code which specifies the type of the response.  This will typically be
     RAD_ACCESS_ACCEPT, RAD_ACCESS_REJECT, or RAD_ACCESS_CHALLENGE.  If no
     valid response is received, rraadd__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt() returns -1.

     As an alternative, if you do not wish to block waiting for a response,
     rraadd__iinniitt__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt() and rraadd__ccoonnttiinnuuee__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt() may be used in‐
     stead.  If a reply is received from the RADIUS server or a timeout oc‐
     curs, these functions return a value as described for rraadd__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt().
     Otherwise, a value of zero is returned and the values pointed to by _f_d
     and _t_v are set to the descriptor and timeout that should be passed to
     select(2).

     rraadd__iinniitt__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt() must be called first, followed by repeated calls
     to rraadd__ccoonnttiinnuuee__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt() as long as a return value of zero is
     given.  Between each call, the application should call select(2), passing
     _*_f_d as a read descriptor and timing out after the interval specified by
     _t_v.  When select(2) returns, rraadd__ccoonnttiinnuuee__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt() should be called
     with _s_e_l_e_c_t_e_d set to a non-zero value if select(2) indicated that the de‐
     scriptor is readable.

     Like RADIUS requests, each response may contain zero or more attributes.
     After a response has been received successfully by rraadd__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt() or
     rraadd__ccoonnttiinnuuee__sseenndd__rreeqquueesstt(), its attributes can be extracted one by one
     using rraadd__ggeett__aattttrr().  Each time rraadd__ggeett__aattttrr() is called, it gets the
     next attribute from the current response, and stores a pointer to the
     data and the length of the data via the reference parameters _d_a_t_a and
     _l_e_n, respectively.  Note that the data resides in the response itself,
     and must not be modified.  A successful call to rraadd__ggeett__aattttrr() returns
     the RADIUS attribute type.  If no more attributes remain in the current
     response, rraadd__ggeett__aattttrr() returns 0.  If an error such as a malformed at‐
     tribute is detected, -1 is returned.

